📂 Module 4.24.2 : Overfitting

  In machine learning, overfitting occurs when a algorithm learns the training dataset too well, capturing noise, random fluctuations, and specific details rather than the general underlying pattern. This typically happens when a model is overly complex relative to the amount and variety of training data available.
When a model overfits, it achieves very high accuracy or exceptionally low error on the training set. However, because it has essentially "memorized" the training examples, it struggles to generalize to new, unseen data. Consequently, its performance drops significantly when evaluated on test datasets. A common example of overfitting is setting an excessively high limit for tree depth or splits (such as MaxNumSplits = 200 in a Decision Tree). The model creates overly specific branch rules that fit every detail of the training data, including background noise.
To prevent or mitigate overfitting, several techniques can be applied: cross-validation, simplifying the model (pruning or constraining hyperparameters), collecting more data, or using ensemble methods like Bagging (e.g., Random Forests) which average multiple decision trees to stabilize predictions and improve generalization performance on unseen test data.

Example Code : 

% Load the tabular data from a CSV file
data = readtable('heart.csv');

% Extract all feature columns (from the first column up to the second-to-last column)
X = data(:, 1:end-1);

% Extract the target label column (last column) and convert it to an array
Y = table2array(data(:, end));

% Partition the dataset into 70% training and 30% testing subsets
cv = cvpartition(Y, 'HoldOut', 0.3);

% Filter the input features to create the training set
XTrain = X(training(cv), :);

% Filter the target labels to create the training set
YTrain = Y(training(cv));

% Filter the input features to create the test set
XTest = X(test(cv), :);

% Filter the target labels to create the test set
YTest = Y(test(cv));

% Train a decision tree with high complexity (demonstrating overfitting)
treeModel = fitctree(XTrain, YTrain, 'MaxNumSplits', 200);

% Calculate accuracy percentage for the single decision tree on training data
trainAcc = mean(predict(treeModel, XTrain) == YTrain) * 100;

% Calculate accuracy percentage for the single decision tree on testing data
testAcc = mean(predict(treeModel, XTest) == YTest) * 100;

% Display single decision tree performance on training and test sets
fprintf('Training Accuracy: %.2f%%\n', trainAcc);
fprintf('Testing Accuracy: %.2f%%\n', testAcc);

% Train an ensemble model using Bagging with 30 learning cycles to combat overfitting
ensModel = fitensemble(XTrain, YTrain, 'Method', 'Bag', 'NumLearningCycles', 30);

% Calculate accuracy percentage for the ensemble model on training data
ensTrainAcc = mean(predict(ensModel, XTrain) == YTrain) * 100;

% Calculate accuracy percentage for the ensemble model on testing data
ensTestAcc = mean(predict(ensModel, XTest) == YTest) * 100;

% Display ensemble model performance on training and test sets
fprintf('Ensemble Training Accuracy: %.2f%%\n', ensTrainAcc);
fprintf('Ensemble Testing Accuracy: %.2f%%\n', ensTestAcc);

